$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM 2.3 Typical Procedure for Pd(OAc)2-Catalyzed C-H Trifluoromethylation. In
a 20 mL sealed tube, N-aryl benzamide 1a (33.7 mg, 0.10 mmol, 1.0
equiv.), 5-(trifluoromethyl)dibenzothiophenium trifluoromethanesulfonate
2a (60.3 mg, 0.15 mmol, 1.5 equiv), Pd(OAc)2 (2.3 mg, 0.01 mmol, 10 mol
%), Cu(OAc)2 (36.4 mg, 0.20 mmol, 2.0 equiv), N-methylformamide L4 (87.6
uL, 1.5 mmol, 15 equiv), and trifluoroacetic acid (74.3 uL, 1.0 mmol, 10
equiv) were dissolved in 1,2-dichloroethane (3 mL) under N2 atmosphere.
The reaction mixture was then tightly capped and stirred for 10 minutes
at room temperature for proper mixing of the reactants, and then heated
at 130 °C with vigorous stirring for 24 hours. The reaction mixture was
then cooled to room temperature, diluted with acetone and filtered
through a small pad of Celite. The filtrate was concentrated in vacuo and
purified by a silica gel packed flash chromatography column, using
acetone/ethyl acetate/hexane (1:1:10) as the eluent.
N-(2,3,5,6-tetrafluoro-4-(trifluoromethyl)phenyl)-2-(trifluoromethyl)benzamide
(3a) 2b. Yield 79%. 1H NMR (400 MHz, acetone-d6) δ 10.06 (s, 1H), 7.89
(d, J = 7.6 Hz, 1H), 7.83-7.76 (m, 3H); 19F NMR (375 MHz, acetone-d6) δ
-56.09 (t, J = 21.6 Hz, 3F), -58.85 (s, 3F), -142.98 to -143.07 (m, 2F),
-143.51 to -143.56 (m, 2F); 13C NMR (100 MHz, acetone-d6) δ 165.66,
134.65, 132.86, 131.30, 129.20, 127.69 (q, J = 32.1 Hz), 127.03 (q, J =
4.9 Hz), 124.11 (q, J = 271.7 Hz).
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM regioselective, optimized on ligand,Lewis acid,ligand loading,TFA
loading, sealed tube used, alternative preparation shown, optimization
study
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Pd(II)-Catalyzed Ortho Trifluoromethylation of Arenes and Insights into
the Coordination Mode of Acidic Amide Directing Groups
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Zhang, Xing-Guo; Dai, Hui-Xiong; Wasa, Masayuki; Yu, Jin-Quan
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Journal of the American Chemical Society (2012), 134(29), 11948-11951

